<p>Sleep is the single most powerful biological enhancer available to humanity. During non-REM slow-wave sleep and REM dreaming phases, the brain's glymphatic system flushes metabolic waste, muscle tissue repairs via human growth hormone surges, and short-term working memories crystallize into long-term neural connections.</p>

<p>Yet over 35% of adults routinely operate on chronic sleep deprivation. Here is what modern sleep laboratories recommend for restoring optimal circadian cycles.</p>

<h2>Circadian Timing: The Sun & Temperature Signals</h2>
<p>Your master circadian clock (the suprachiasmatic nucleus) relies on two primary external zeitgebers: photons and ambient temperature.</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Morning Sunlight Exposure:</strong> Get 10–15 minutes of direct sunlight into your eyes within 30 minutes of waking. This triggers an early cortisol pulse and sets an internal timer for natural melatonin release 14 hours later.</li>
    <li><strong>Thermal Regulation:</strong> Human core body temperature must drop by roughly 1–2 degrees Fahrenheit to initiate sleep. Keep bedroom temperatures between 65°F and 68°F (18°C–20°C).</li>
    <li><strong>The 3-2-1 Caffeine & Food Rule:</strong> No heavy meals 3 hours before bed; no liquids 2 hours before bed; zero caffeine 10 hours before your target sleep time.</li>
</ul>

